'''Deaf View Image Art''', abbreviated as '''De'VIA''', is a genre of visual art that intentionally represents the Deaf experience and [[Deaf culture]]. Although De'VIA works have been created throughout history, the term was first defined and recognized as an art genre in 1989.<ref name="Curriculum">{{cite web|title=What is De'VIA|url=https://deviacurr.wordpress.com/devia-curr/what-is-devia/|website=De'VIA Curriculum|access-date=26 February 2018|date=14 July 2015}}</ref> In 1989, a group of nine Deaf artists gathered at [[Gallaudet University]] shortly before the Deaf Way arts festival was being held there. Led by [[Betty G. Miller]], known as the Mother of De'VIA,<ref name="Durr">{{cite journal|last1=Durr|first1=Patricia|title=De'VIA: Investigating Deaf Visual Art|journal=Visual Anthropology Review|date=2006|url=http://scholarworks.rit.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1424&context=article}}</ref> and Paul Johnston, these artists created a manifesto detailing what De'VIA is and what it includes.<ref name="DeafArt">{{cite web|title=What is Deaf Art?|url=http://www.deafart.org/Deaf_Art_/deaf_art_.html|website=www.deafart.org}}</ref> Since its official designation as a genre, De'VIA has helped to introduce the Deaf experience to the artistic world and give a new platform to the Deaf community.<ref name="Lifeprint">{{cite web|last1=Zelinski|first1=Karly|title=Deaf Visual Arts: De'VIA (Deaf View/ Image Art)|url=http://www.lifeprint.com/asl101/topics/devia.htm|website=American Sign Language University|access-date=18 March 2018}}</ref>

